    hero: {
      title: 'MATGO (KOREAN GO-STOP)',
      subtitle: 'Card Game',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Matgo (Korean Go-Stop)',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Matgo is the quintessential two-player variant of 'Go-Stop', a popular Korean fishing game played with Hwatu ('Flower Cards'). Using a 48-card deck where each group of four represents a month/flower, players attempt to capture cards to form scoring combinations (Yaku). The name 'Matgo' distinguishes the 2-player competitive style from the 3-player 'Go-Stop'. It is arguably the most popular mobile and online game in South Korea.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Hwatu cards were introduced to Korea by Japan (derived from Hanafuda) in the late 19th century. Go-Stop and Matgo evolved as Korean-specific rule sets in the mid-20th century.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Matgo is played by exactly two players: one dealer (seon) and one opponent. Play proceeds counterclockwise from the dealer. The two-player variant uses a higher point target (typically seven) than three-player Go-Stop (typically three).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '48 cards: 12 months, 4 cards each. Themes: Pine (Jan), Plum (Feb), Cherry (Mar)... Paulownia (Dec).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '48 cards: 12 months, 4 cards each. Themes: Pine (Jan), Plum (Feb), Cherry (Mar)... Paulownia (Dec).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player receives 10 cards. 8 cards are placed face-up on the table.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Target 5 points: Agreed target is 5 points instead of 7; faster games.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Three-player (Go-Stop): Standard Go-Stop with 3 players: 7 cards each, 6 on table, target usually 3 points.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'With jokers: Add one or more jokers to the deck; jokers have special capture or scoring rules by table agreement.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
